Q: What is Indexing in SQL?

🔍 What is Indexing?
Indexing is a database optimization technique to find rows faster without scanning the whole table.

Correction (important):
Indexing is NOT “another name for non-clustered index.”
- Indexing = the general concept
- Clustered / Non-clustered = types of indexes

📘 Simple definition:
Like a book index — jump to the page instead of reading every page.

🔧 How it works (typical secondary / non-clustered index)
CREATE INDEX creates a separate sorted structure on column value(s).
In InnoDB secondary indexes, leaves store indexed column(s) + Primary Key,
then PK is used to fetch the full row from the clustered index.

Query:
SELECT * FROM employees WHERE name = 'John';
→ search index on name → get PK → fetch row (unless covering index).

🛠️ Example:
CREATE INDEX idx_employee_name ON employees(name);

Composite example (order matters: leftmost prefix):
CREATE INDEX idx_name_dept ON employees(name, department_id);
-- Helps: WHERE name = ?
-- Helps: WHERE name = ? AND department_id = ?
-- Does NOT help well: WHERE department_id = ? alone

✅ Benefits
- Faster SELECT with WHERE / JOIN / ORDER BY / GROUP BY (when index is usable)
- Can enforce uniqueness (UNIQUE index / PK)

⚠️ Downsides
- Slower INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE (indexes must be maintained)
- Extra disk/memory
- Too many indexes can hurt write-heavy workloads
- Wrong/unused indexes waste space

📚 Types of indexes

| Type          | Description                                              |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Single-column | Index on one column                                      |
| Composite     | Index on multiple columns (leftmost prefix matters)      |
| Unique        | No duplicate key values                                  |
| Full-text     | Text search                                              |
| Clustered     | Table rows organized by this key (one per table; PK in InnoDB) |
| Non-clustered | Separate structure pointing to row via PK (InnoDB)       |

Related note:
See → indexing (Clustered VS Non-Cluster )

When indexes help most:
- High selectivity (many distinct values), e.g. email, id
When less useful:
- Low selectivity columns alone (e.g. gender true/false) — often full scan is fine

Interview one-liner:
“An index is a sorted lookup structure that speeds reads; clustered holds the rows (PK in InnoDB), non-clustered/secondary points to them via the PK,
at the cost of slower writes and extra storage.”
